        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"hamsun-victoria-9c9ba178c9955a44\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Victoria’s retrospective written avowal presents her enduring past love for Johannes as true. It neither undertakes a future relational commitment nor functions primarily as a reflex-like discharge: the letter is deliberately composed to tell him what she could not previously show.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The invocation “Du gode Gud” and the deathbed intensity make the delivery highly expressive in an ordinary sense, but the passage supplies no independent evidence that the core love words are an affective reflex rather than an emotionally charged avowal.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Victoria writes the avowal in a final letter that Johannes reads after her death; the narrative reproduces the written utterance.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Facing death, Victoria abandons her former shame and reserve and explicitly tells Johannes that she has loved him. She describes the letter as a way of being near him at the end, while also insisting that everything is now too late and that she has no right to summon him. These motives and the intense deathbed framing deepen the avowal but do not turn it into a relational undertaking or independently establish reflexive E-force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Du gode Gud, De skulde vite hvor jeg har elsket Dem, Johannes” (“Dear God, if only you knew how I have loved you, Johannes”).\",\"supports\":\"The perfect-form clause explicitly presents Victoria’s loving state across the past as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“nu når jeg skal dø og altsammen er forsent da skriver jeg til Dem endnu en gang og sier Dem det” (“now, when I am going to die and everything is too late, I am writing to you once again and telling you so”).\",\"supports\":\"Victoria characterizes the act as deliberate written disclosure of her love; the assertion that everything is too late weighs against commitment or undertaking force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She says she could not previously show her love because much stood in her way, especially her own nature.\",\"supports\":\"Distinguishes the present explicit avowal from earlier unexpressed feeling and reinforces its truth-conditional disclosure function.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Later in the letter she says she has written on it many times that day and still has not said a thousandth of what she wanted to say.\",\"supports\":\"The sustained, repeatedly composed letter provides evidence against construing the highlighted clause as an unmediated reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is annotated as affirmative perfect form (“jeg har elsket Dem”) near the end of Knut Hamsun’s Victoria.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the retrospective grammatical presentation relevant to the strong T reading.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core as a retrospective avowal. The emotional deathbed setting is adequately represented as context and intensity, without requiring P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
